M.ABSTRACT=("This study examines changes in total and groundwater storage across Thailand's 22 principal river basins. It addresses three objectives: measure storage trends, assess the climate-related baseline and the remaining residual, and provide a reproducible decision-support website. GRACE and GRACE-FO observations provide total water storage anomalies (TWSA). Groundwater storage anomalies (GWSA) are calculated by subtracting soil, surface, canopy and snow water anomalies. All terms use compatible units, reference periods and spatial coverage. National TWSA decreased by 7.51 mm/year through September 2024, while GWSA decreased by 6.84 mm/year through September 2023. Basin rankings change substantially between processing chains, so local rankings need caution. A naturalized WaterGAP simulation provides the baseline without the direct human activities represented in its paired historical simulation. Statistical models reconstruct this baseline from rainfall, potential evapotranspiration and climate memory; they are tested on 2016–2023 data that were excluded from fitting and model selection. GLDAS is used to compare matching soil, canopy and snow compartments. The observation-minus-baseline residual contains both human effects and errors in the observations and models. Groundwater levels and independent reservoir records therefore provide supporting checks rather than a direct measurement of the full human component. Groundwater agreement is weak and changes with the selected wells and years. The season- and trend-adjusted national reservoir correlation is 0.439 across 73 scene-supported months. The main finding is a national storage decline, with greater uncertainty in basin rankings and human attribution. The report presents the results through 21 figures, an interactive website and code that can be rerun independently.")

